export const CELLS = [
  {"i":0,"type":"markdown","src":"# CITS5017 Project 2: Forecasting Perth Temperatures with GRUs, and Synthesising Them with a VAE\n\n**Name:** James Wigfield\n\n**Student number:** 23334375\n\nThe data is monthly mean minimum and maximum temperatures from five Bureau of Meteorology stations around Perth. In part (i) I train three GRU models to forecast the next 12 months from the previous 72: one that predicts all 12 months in one go, one that predicts a single month and is rolled forward, and an encoder-decoder. I then compare how the last two hold up over longer horizons and on a station they never saw. In part (ii) I train a variational autoencoder on 84-month maximum temperature sequences and look at whether the sequences it makes up are believable.\n\nEverything was run locally in a conda environment with TensorFlow 2.21.0 and Keras 3.15.1 (the VAE code needs Keras 3) on an NVIDIA RTX 5070 Ti.","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"CITS5017 Project 2: Forecasting Perth Temperatures with GRUs, and Synthesising Them with a VAE","id":"dlp2-h-cits5017-project-2-forecasting-perth-temperatures-with-grus-"}]},
  {"i":1,"type":"markdown","src":"## 1. Setup\n\nThe seed is fixed and TensorFlow's deterministic ops are switched on so that re-running the notebook reproduces the saved numbers on the same hardware. A different GPU or a CPU run gives slightly different numbers, so the values quoted in the text are the ones from the saved run. The log-level line hides TensorFlow's own log messages, which on my GPU include a stream of harmless warnings about compiling kernels for a card newer than the TensorFlow build.","headings":[{"level":2,"text":"1. Setup","id":"dlp2-h-1-setup"}]},
  {"i":2,"type":"code","src":"import os\nos.environ[\"TF_CPP_MIN_LOG_LEVEL\"] = \"3\"     # hide TensorFlow's log lines (lots of harmless GPU kernel warnings here)\n\nimport time\nimport numpy as np\nimport tensorflow as tf\nfrom tensorflow import keras\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nprint(\"TensorFlow\", tf.__version__, \"| Keras\", keras.__version__, \"| NumPy\", np.__version__)\nprint(\"GPUs:\", tf.config.list_physical_devices(\"GPU\"))\n\nkeras.utils.set_random_seed(42)\ntf.config.experimental.enable_op_determinism()   # so a re-run gives the same numbers","ec":1,"outputs":[{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"TensorFlow 2.21.0 | Keras 3.15.1 | NumPy 2.5.2\nGPUs: [PhysicalDevice(name='/physical_device:GPU:0', device_type='GPU')]\n","lines":2,"noise":false,"tfLines":0}]},
  {"i":3,"type":"markdown","src":"## 2. Loading the data\n\nThe pickle holds three TensorFlow tensors, one per split, and I keep them as tensors throughout. Each row is a run of consecutive months from one weather station, and the last axis holds two channels: index 0 is the monthly mean minimum temperature and index 1 the monthly mean maximum, both in degrees C. The training set has 1522 sequences of 84 months from three stations on the coastal plain (Perth Airport, Jandakot Aero and Perth Metro) and is already shuffled. The validation set has 313 sequences of 84 months from Swanbourne, right on the coast, and the test set has 297 sequences of 96 months from Bickley, up in the hills east of the city. The extra 12 months in the test set only enter the descriptive statistics in the next cell and the longer horizons in section 9; no model sees them before then.","headings":[{"level":2,"text":"2. Loading the data","id":"dlp2-h-2-loading-the-data"}]},
  {"i":4,"type":"code","src":"def load_data(picklefile):\n    import pickle\n    f = open(picklefile, \"rb\")\n    data = pickle.load(f)\n    f.close()\n    return data\n\n(train_seq, val_seq, test_seq) = load_data(\"temperatures-2026.pkl\")\nprint(train_seq.shape, val_seq.shape, test_seq.shape)","ec":2,"outputs":[{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"(1522, 84, 2) (313, 84, 2) (297, 96, 2)\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0}]},
  {"i":5,"type":"code","src":"for name, seq in [(\"train\", train_seq), (\"val\", val_seq), (\"test\", test_seq)]:\n    mean = tf.reduce_mean(seq, axis=(0, 1))\n    std = tf.math.reduce_std(seq, axis=(0, 1))\n    print(f\"{name:5s} {type(seq).__name__} {seq.dtype.name}  min: mean {mean[0]:.1f} std {std[0]:.1f}   \"\n          f\"max: mean {mean[1]:.1f} std {std[1]:.1f}  (deg C)\")\n\n# consecutive sequences in the validation and test sets are the same months shifted by one\nprint(\"val windows overlap:\", bool(tf.reduce_all(val_seq[1:, :-1] == val_seq[:-1, 1:])))\nprint(\"test windows overlap:\", bool(tf.reduce_all(test_seq[1:, :-1] == test_seq[:-1, 1:])))","ec":3,"outputs":[{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"train EagerTensor float32  min: mean 12.2 std 3.8   max: mean 24.7 std 5.1  (deg C)\nval   EagerTensor float32  min: mean 14.0 std 3.3   max: mean 24.1 std 4.4  (deg C)\ntest  EagerTensor float32  min: mean 11.3 std 3.2   max: mean 22.6 std 5.7  (deg C)\nval windows overlap: True\ntest windows overlap: True\n","lines":5,"noise":false,"tfLines":0}]},
  {"i":6,"type":"markdown","src":"The three sets are not interchangeable. Bickley averages 11.3 degrees for the minimum and 22.6 for the maximum against 12.2 and 24.7 in the training set, so it is cooler on both channels, and its maximums swing more (standard deviation 5.7 against 5.1). Swanbourne's minimums are warmer (14.0) and both of its channels vary less than the training set's (3.3 and 4.4 against 3.8 and 5.1), which is the sea moderating its temperatures. That matters later, since a model trained on the three training stations has to extrapolate a little to predict Bickley.\n\nThe overlap check shows that each validation or test sequence is the previous one shifted along by one month, so the 313 validation sequences are sliding windows over a single 396-month Swanbourne record and the 297 test sequences are windows over a 392-month Bickley record. The training set is the same kind of thing, only shuffled. The brief gives 987 months for Perth Airport and 392 for Jandakot Aero, and Perth Metro also starts in 1994; 904 + 309 + 309 = 1522 windows of 84 months is exactly the number of training sequences, so Perth Metro must have 392 months as well. None of the sets is a collection of independent samples. A test MAE is really the error over one station's history rather than over 297 separate cases, and the training set holds about 1770 distinct months rather than 1522 independent seven-year windows. Consecutive windows also start one month apart, so the start months cover the whole calendar and the 12 target months can begin anywhere in the annual cycle, which is useful variety for the models."},
  {"i":7,"type":"markdown","src":"# Part (i): Forecasting minimum and maximum temperatures\n\nThree GRU models forecast the next 12 months of minimum and maximum temperatures from the previous 72, and section 9 compares two of them over longer horizons and on the unseen test station.","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Part (i): Forecasting minimum and maximum temperatures","id":"dlp2-h-part-i-forecasting-minimum-and-maximum-temperatures"}]},
  {"i":8,"type":"markdown","src":"## 3. Splitting into input and target sequences\n\nsplit_sequences slices each sequence into the first 72 months as the input and the following 12 as the target, with plain tensor slicing so everything stays a TensorFlow tensor. The lengths are arguments because section 9 reuses the function with longer targets, up to the full 24 months the test set allows. For now all three sets give X of shape (N, 72, 2) and y of shape (N, 12, 2).","headings":[{"level":2,"text":"3. Splitting into input and target sequences","id":"dlp2-h-3-splitting-into-input-and-target-sequences"}]},
  {"i":9,"type":"code","src":"INPUT_LENGTH = 72\nTARGET_LENGTH = 12\n\ndef split_sequences(seq, input_length=INPUT_LENGTH, target_length=TARGET_LENGTH):\n    \"\"\"Split sequences into input (X) and target (y) tensors.\n\n    X is the first input_length months of every sequence and y the target_length months after them.\n    \"\"\"\n    X = seq[:, :input_length]\n    y = seq[:, input_length:input_length + target_length]\n    return X, y\n\nX_train, y_train = split_sequences(train_seq)\nX_val, y_val = split_sequences(val_seq)\nX_test, y_test = split_sequences(test_seq)\nfor name, X, y in [(\"train\", X_train, y_train), (\"val\", X_val, y_val), (\"test\", X_test, y_test)]:\n    print(f\"{name:5s} X {tuple(X.shape)}  y {tuple(y.shape)}\")","ec":4,"outputs":[{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"train X (1522, 72, 2)  y (1522, 12, 2)\nval   X (313, 72, 2)  y (313, 12, 2)\ntest  X (297, 72, 2)  y (297, 12, 2)\n","lines":3,"noise":false,"tfLines":0}]},
  {"i":10,"type":"markdown","src":"## 4. Visualising the sequences\n\ndisplay_temperatures draws three sequences side by side, with the minimum in blue and the maximum in red, the input months as solid lines, the target months dashed, and a grey line where the input ends. The optional y_pred argument adds predictions in the same colours, and indices lets me show the same three test sequences for every model so they can be compared on the same examples. Targets and predictions carry markers so that a single-month target, which model 2 needs, is still visible. The three sequences are picked with NumPy's random generator, which the seed in section 1 fixes.","headings":[{"level":2,"text":"4. Visualising the sequences","id":"dlp2-h-4-visualising-the-sequences"}]},
  {"i":11,"type":"code","src":"def display_temperatures(X, y, y_pred=None, indices=None, title=None):\n    \"\"\"Plot 3 sequences from X with their target months in 1x3 subplots.\n\n    X, y: input and target tensors, shapes (N, input_len, 2) and (N, target_len, 2).\n    y_pred: optional predictions shaped like y, drawn over the targets.\n    indices: which 3 sequences to show (default: 3 chosen at random).\n    \"\"\"\n    if indices is None:\n        indices = np.random.choice(len(X), size=3, replace=False)\n    n_in, n_out = X.shape[1], y.shape[1]\n    t_in = np.arange(1, n_in + 1)\n    t_out = np.arange(n_in + 1, n_in + n_out + 1)\n    fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 3, figsize=(14, 4.2), sharey=True)\n    for ax, i in zip(axes, indices):\n        x_i, y_i = np.asarray(X[i]), np.asarray(y[i])\n        for ch, name, colour in [(0, \"min\", \"tab:blue\"), (1, \"max\", \"tab:red\")]:\n            ax.plot(t_in, x_i[:, ch], color=colour, lw=1, label=f\"{name} input\")\n            ax.plot(t_out, y_i[:, ch], color=colour, lw=1, ls=\"--\", marker=\"o\", ms=3, label=f\"{name} target\")\n            if y_pred is not None:\n                ax.plot(t_out, np.asarray(y_pred[i])[:, ch], color=colour, lw=1.2, ls=\":\",\n                        marker=\"x\", ms=5, label=f\"{name} predicted\")\n        ax.axvline(n_in + 0.5, color=\"grey\", lw=0.8)        # where the input ends\n        ax.set_title(f\"sequence {i}\")\n        ax.set_xlabel(\"month\")\n    axes[0].set_ylabel(\"temperature (deg C)\")\n    handles, labels = axes[0].get_legend_handles_labels()\n    fig.legend(handles, labels, loc=\"lower center\", ncol=len(labels), fontsize=9, frameon=False)\n    if title:\n        fig.suptitle(title)\n    plt.tight_layout(rect=(0, 0.06, 1, 1))\n    plt.show()","ec":5,"outputs":[]},
  {"i":12,"type":"code","src":"display_temperatures(X_train, y_train, title=\"Training set (Perth Airport, Jandakot Aero, Perth Metro)\")\ndisplay_temperatures(X_val, y_val, title=\"Validation set (Swanbourne)\")\ndisplay_temperatures(X_test, y_test, title=\"Test set (Bickley)\")","ec":6,"outputs":[{"kind":"display_data","png":"cell12_0.png","w":1390,"h":425,"text":"<Figure size 1400x420 with 3 Axes>","mimes":["image/png","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","png":"cell12_1.png","w":1390,"h":425,"text":"<Figure size 1400x420 with 3 Axes>","mimes":["image/png","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","png":"cell12_2.png","w":1390,"h":425,"text":"<Figure size 1400x420 with 3 Axes>","mimes":["image/png","text/plain"]}]},
  {"i":13,"type":"markdown","src":"The annual cycle dominates every sequence: six full cycles in the 72 input months, with summer maximums of 30 to 35 degrees and minimums of 15 to 20, and winter maximums of 15 to 19 and minimums of 5 to 10. On top of that there is a lot of month-to-month noise, sharp one-month spikes in summer, and a gap between the two channels that is wider in summer than in winter. The Bickley test sequences sit visibly lower on both channels, with winter maximums close to 15, while Swanbourne's minimums rarely drop below 8. Because each window starts in a different month, the 12 target months can start anywhere in the cycle, and in some of the examples they begin on a summer peak and in others in the middle of winter."},
  {"i":14,"type":"markdown","src":"## 5. Shared helpers\n\nScaling. The GRU layers use tanh, the default activation, which saturates once its input gets much past plus or minus 2. Feeding it raw temperatures of 5 to 36 degrees makes training crawl: in my experiments a tanh GRU on raw temperatures was still at a validation MAE of 3.5 degrees when early stopping ended the run, worse than repeating last year. The fix is the same one used for images, a fixed affine scaling that is identical for every sequence and every month. I subtract the training mean and divide by the training standard deviation of each channel (12.2 and 3.8 for the minimum, 24.7 and 5.1 for the maximum), which maps the temperatures to z-scores of roughly -2 to 2. Nothing else is done to the data, so the shape of every sequence is preserved in the same way that dividing an image by 255 preserves the image.\n\nRather than scaling the tensors by hand, I put the scaling inside the models with a pair of Normalization layers: one at the input that converts degrees to z-scores and one at the output, with invert=True, that converts the predictions back. The statistics come from X_train only; every model gets its own pair of layers built from the same two numbers, and model 3 shares one pair between its encoder and decoder inputs. The pay-off is that every tensor outside a model stays in degrees C: the MSE loss is in degrees squared, the MAE metric Keras tracks is in degrees, and the recursive and encoder-decoder loops can feed predictions straight back in without converting anything.\n\nActivation. The alternative is ReLU in the GRU layers, whose hidden state is then unbounded and can carry raw temperatures directly. I tried it in the tuning runs. With the same scaling it matched tanh within seed noise (about 1.0 degrees validation MAE for the 12-month model), and on raw temperatures it was worse (1.5 degrees). It was also far slower on my GPU, about 20 seconds per epoch rather than one, because only a tanh GRU runs on the cuDNN kernel. So the GRU layers use tanh on scaled inputs.\n\nTraining. All three models use Adam and the MSE loss, early stopping on the validation loss with patience 20 and the best weights restored, at most 100 epochs, and a batch size of 32 (48 steps per epoch). I chose MSE over MAE because its gradients shrink as the error shrinks, which gave smoother validation curves; MAE and Huber reached the same validation MAE within noise. The learning rate was picked on validation MAE. Models 1 and 3 use 0.003, because they were still improving after 100 epochs at the default 0.001 and became unstable at 0.01; model 2 uses 0.01, where it was better. Patience 20 rather than 10 matters because the validation loss is noisy and a couple of my early runs stopped on a bump. The tuning runs themselves are not in the notebook, only the final settings, and the tuning numbers quoted in the text are from those runs.\n\njit_compile=False is passed to every compile call. On my GPU the default XLA compilation fails on the first training step for some of these models, and the flag turns it off without changing anything else. evaluate_mae is the mean absolute difference over every sequence, month and channel, in degrees. The three test sequences used in every model's plots are drawn once here.","headings":[{"level":2,"text":"5. Shared helpers","id":"dlp2-h-5-shared-helpers"}]},
  {"i":15,"type":"code","src":"LEARNING_RATE = 3e-3\nPATIENCE = 20\nBATCH_SIZE = 32\nGRU_UNITS = 16\n\n# per-channel mean and std of the training inputs: the only statistics the scaling uses\nMEAN = tf.reduce_mean(X_train, axis=(0, 1))\nSTD = tf.math.reduce_std(X_train, axis=(0, 1))\nprint(f\"training mean (min, max): {MEAN.numpy().round(2)}  std: {STD.numpy().round(2)}\")\n\ndef scaling_layers():\n    \"\"\"A pair of Normalization layers: deg C -> z-scores on the way in, z-scores -> deg C on the way out.\"\"\"\n    norm = keras.layers.Normalization(axis=-1, mean=MEAN.numpy(), variance=STD.numpy() ** 2)\n    denorm = keras.layers.Normalization(axis=-1, mean=MEAN.numpy(), variance=STD.numpy() ** 2, invert=True)\n    return norm, denorm\n\ndef build_gru_model(target_length, units=GRU_UNITS, name=None):\n    \"\"\"Two tanh GRU layers -> Dense -> Reshape to (target_length, 2), with the scaling layers at both ends.\"\"\"\n    norm, denorm = scaling_layers()\n    return keras.Sequential([\n        keras.Input(shape=(None, 2)),                   # any number of months, min and max channels\n        norm,\n        keras.layers.GRU(units, activation=\"tanh\", return_sequences=True),\n        keras.layers.GRU(units, activation=\"tanh\"),\n        keras.layers.Dense(target_length * 2),\n        keras.layers.Reshape((target_length, 2)),\n        denorm,\n    ], name=name)\n\ndef train_model(model, X_train, y_train, X_val, y_val, epochs=100, learning_rate=LEARNING_RATE,\n                patience=PATIENCE, loss=\"mse\", metrics=(\"mae\",)):\n    \"\"\"Compile with Adam and fit with early stopping on val_loss (best weights restored), verbose=0.\n\n    Returns the History and prints where training stopped and which epoch's weights were kept.\n    \"\"\"\n    model.compile(optimizer=keras.optimizers.Adam(learning_rate), loss=loss, metrics=list(metrics),\n                  jit_compile=False)\n    early_stopping = keras.callbacks.EarlyStopping(monitor=\"val_loss\", patience=patience,\n                                                   restore_best_weights=True)\n    start = time.perf_counter()\n    history = model.fit(X_train, y_train, validation_data=(X_val, y_val), epochs=epochs,\n                        batch_size=BATCH_SIZE, callbacks=[early_stopping], verbose=0)\n    h = history.history\n    best = int(np.argmin(h[\"val_loss\"])) + 1\n    print(f\"stopped after {len(history.epoch)} epochs ({time.perf_counter() - start:.0f} s), kept the weights \"\n          f\"from epoch {best}: val_loss {h['val_loss'][best - 1]:.4f}, val_{metrics[0]} {h['val_' + metrics[0]][best - 1]:.3f}\")\n    return history\n\ndef plot_learning_curve(history, title=None, metric=\"mae\", metric_label=\"MAE (deg C)\",\n                        loss_label=\"MSE loss (deg C squared)\"):\n    \"\"\"Training and validation loss and metric per epoch, with the restored epoch marked.\"\"\"\n    h = history.history\n    epochs = np.arange(1, len(h[\"loss\"]) + 1)\n    best = int(np.argmin(h[\"val_loss\"])) + 1\n    fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(11, 3.8))\n    for ax, key, label in zip(axes, [\"loss\", metric], [loss_label, metric_label]):\n        ax.plot(epochs, h[key], label=\"training\")\n        ax.plot(epochs, h[\"val_\" + key], label=\"validation\")\n        ax.axvline(best, color=\"grey\", ls=\":\", label=f\"best epoch ({best})\")\n        ax.set(xlabel=\"epoch\", ylabel=label)\n        ax.legend()\n    if title:\n        fig.suptitle(title)\n    plt.tight_layout()\n    plt.show()\n\ndef evaluate_mae(y_true, y_pred):\n    \"\"\"Mean absolute error in deg C over every sequence, month and channel.\"\"\"\n    return float(tf.reduce_mean(tf.abs(y_true - y_pred)))\n\n# the same 3 random test sequences are shown for every model\nSHOW = np.random.choice(len(X_test), size=3, replace=False)","ec":7,"outputs":[{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"training mean (min, max): [12.22 24.69]  std: [3.8 5.1]\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0}]},
  {"i":16,"type":"markdown","src":"## 6. Model 1: two-layer GRU predicting 12 months at once\n\nModel 1 is the plainest design that fits the brief: two GRU layers of 16 units, the first returning its whole output sequence to the second, then a Dense layer of 24 units reshaped to (12, 2), all sitting between the two scaling layers. The second GRU returns only its final hidden state, so the Dense layer sees a 16-number summary of the 72 months and maps it straight to 12 months of minimums and maximums. Sixteen units is the most the brief suggests, and when I tried 8 units it was worse (about 1.4 degrees on validation rather than 1.0). The input length is left as None so the same builder works for any number of months. The model has 3,000 parameters, which is modest next to the training data even allowing for the overlap between windows: three station records of about 1770 months in total, each month a target for up to 12 different windows.","headings":[{"level":2,"text":"6. Model 1: two-layer GRU predicting 12 months at once","id":"dlp2-h-6-model-1-two-layer-gru-predicting-12-months-at-once"}]},
  {"i":17,"type":"code","src":"model1 = build_gru_model(TARGET_LENGTH, name=\"model1\")\nmodel1.summary()","ec":8,"outputs":[{"kind":"display_data","text":"Model: \"model1\"\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":"┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓\n┃ Layer (type)                    ┃ Output Shape           ┃       Param # ┃\n┡━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┩\n│ normalization (Normalization)   │ (None, None, 2)        │             0 │\n├─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤\n│ gru (GRU)                       │ (None, None, 16)       │           960 │\n├─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤\n│ gru_1 (GRU)                     │ (None, 16)             │         1,632 │\n├─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤\n│ dense (Dense)                   │ (None, 24)             │           408 │\n├─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤\n│ reshape (Reshape)               │ (None, 12, 2)          │             0 │\n├─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤\n│ normalization_1 (Normalization) │ (None, 12, 2)          │             0 │\n└─────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────┴───────────────┘\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Total params: 3,000 (11.72 KB)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Trainable params: 3,000 (11.72 KB)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Non-trainable params: 0 (0.00 B)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]}]},
  {"i":18,"type":"code","src":"history1 = train_model(model1, X_train, y_train, X_val, y_val)\nplot_learning_curve(history1, title=\"Model 1\")","ec":9,"outputs":[{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"stopped after 66 epochs (66 s), kept the weights from epoch 46: val_loss 1.5473, val_mae 0.988\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0},{"kind":"display_data","png":"cell18_0.png","w":1090,"h":377,"text":"<Figure size 1100x380 with 2 Axes>","mimes":["image/png","text/plain"]}]},
  {"i":19,"type":"markdown","src":"Training stopped after 66 epochs and restored epoch 46, where the validation loss bottomed out at 1.55 degrees squared, a validation MAE of 0.988 degrees. The training loss drops below 2.5 within five epochs and then creeps down to 1.5. The validation loss starts lower than training in the first epoch, which is just the training loss being averaged over an epoch in which the weights were still changing fast. It then sits on a shelf around 2.8 to 3.0 between epochs 10 and 22 before catching the training curve at about epoch 45. At the best epoch the two curves lie almost on top of each other, so there is no real train-validation gap. After epoch 46 the validation loss drifts up to about 1.7 while training keeps inching down, which is mild overfitting, and early stopping is what keeps the model at the better point rather than the slightly worse end point."},
  {"i":20,"type":"code","src":"y_pred1 = model1(X_test, training=False)\nprint(f\"Model 1 test MAE (12 months): {evaluate_mae(y_test, y_pred1):.3f} deg C\")\ndisplay_temperatures(X_test, y_test, y_pred1, indices=SHOW, title=\"Model 1: 12-month predictions on the test set\")","ec":10,"outputs":[{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"Model 1 test MAE (12 months): 1.250 deg C\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0},{"kind":"display_data","png":"cell20_0.png","w":1390,"h":425,"text":"<Figure size 1400x420 with 3 Axes>","mimes":["image/png","text/plain"]}]},
  {"i":21,"type":"markdown","src":"On the test set the MAE is 1.250 degrees, compared with 0.988 on validation, so the model loses about a quarter of a degree moving from Swanbourne to Bickley. The plots show why. The shape of the year is right in all three sequences, the summer peaks of the maximum are within two or three degrees, and the minimums are within a degree or two most months. The winter maximums are the problem: in sequence 95 the model predicts about 17.5 to 19 degrees for the winter trough where Bickley recorded 14 to 16, and the same thing happens, less strongly, in the other two. Bickley's winters are cooler than anything in the training set and the model only partly adjusts to that from the input."},
  {"i":22,"type":"markdown","src":"## 7. Model 2: two-layer GRU predicting 1 month, rolled out to 12\n\nModel 2 is model 1 with the output cut down to a single month: the same two GRU(16) layers, then a Dense layer of 2 units reshaped to (1, 2), so it predicts the minimum and maximum for month 73 only and has 2,626 parameters. It trains on y_train[:, :1], the first target month, and validates on the same slice of y_val. Because the architecture is shared, the builder from section 5 is reused with target_length=1 and the training and plotting helpers are unchanged. The learning rate is 0.01 rather than 0.003. In the tuning runs this model was consistently better at the higher rate (over five seeds, a one-month validation MAE of about 0.88 compared with 0.90, and 1.01 compared with 1.04 once rolled out to 12 months), while model 1 fell apart at 0.01.","headings":[{"level":2,"text":"7. Model 2: two-layer GRU predicting 1 month, rolled out to 12","id":"dlp2-h-7-model-2-two-layer-gru-predicting-1-month-rolled-out-to-12"}]},
  {"i":23,"type":"code","src":"model2 = build_gru_model(1, name=\"model2\")\nmodel2.summary()","ec":11,"outputs":[{"kind":"display_data","text":"Model: \"model2\"\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":"┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓\n┃ Layer (type)                    ┃ Output Shape           ┃       Param # ┃\n┡━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┩\n│ normalization_2 (Normalization) │ (None, None, 2)        │             0 │\n├─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤\n│ gru_2 (GRU)                     │ (None, None, 16)       │           960 │\n├─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤\n│ gru_3 (GRU)                     │ (None, 16)             │         1,632 │\n├─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤\n│ dense_1 (Dense)                 │ (None, 2)              │            34 │\n├─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤\n│ reshape_1 (Reshape)             │ (None, 1, 2)           │             0 │\n├─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤\n│ normalization_3 (Normalization) │ (None, 1, 2)           │             0 │\n└─────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────┴───────────────┘\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Total params: 2,626 (10.26 KB)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Trainable params: 2,626 (10.26 KB)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Non-trainable params: 0 (0.00 B)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]}]},
  {"i":24,"type":"code","src":"# only the first target month is used: shapes (1522, 1, 2) and (313, 1, 2)\n# this model trains best with a higher learning rate than model 1\nhistory2 = train_model(model2, X_train, y_train[:, :1], X_val, y_val[:, :1], learning_rate=1e-2)\nplot_learning_curve(history2, title=\"Model 2 (1 month ahead)\")","ec":12,"outputs":[{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"stopped after 40 epochs (40 s), kept the weights from epoch 20: val_loss 1.2393, val_mae 0.882\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0},{"kind":"display_data","png":"cell24_0.png","w":1090,"h":377,"text":"<Figure size 1100x380 with 2 Axes>","mimes":["image/png","text/plain"]}]},
  {"i":25,"type":"markdown","src":"Model 2 has the least tidy curve of the three. Early stopping ended it after 40 epochs and kept the weights from epoch 20, where the validation loss was 1.24 degrees squared and the validation MAE 0.882 degrees. Up to that point the validation loss sits below the training loss.\n\nTwo things explain that. Swanbourne's maximums vary less overall than the training stations' (standard deviation 4.4 against 5.1), so its next month is easier to predict. And the training loss is averaged over an epoch of changing weights. After epoch 20 the validation loss turns up sharply and has doubled to about 2.5 by epoch 40, while the training loss, after a small bump of its own around epochs 25 to 27, keeps falling to about 1.45. That is partly an optimisation wobble at the high learning rate and then plain overfitting, with the gap between the curves widening to the end. Early stopping restores the weights from the minimum, so the saved model is not the overfitted one, but the curve is a warning that this model is sensitive to when it stops.\n\nI tried the usual remedies in the tuning runs. Input dropout of 0.1 to 0.3 in the GRU layers made the one-month validation MAE worse every time (0.93 or more, where the same seeds without it gave 0.86 to 0.91), and the lower learning rate of 0.003 gave a flatter curve but a worse validation MAE. So I kept this setting and lean on early stopping."},
  {"i":26,"type":"code","src":"y_pred2_1 = model2(X_test, training=False)\nprint(f\"Model 2 test MAE (1 month): {evaluate_mae(y_test[:, :1], y_pred2_1):.3f} deg C\")\ndisplay_temperatures(X_test, y_test[:, :1], y_pred2_1, indices=SHOW, title=\"Model 2: 1-month predictions on the test set\")","ec":13,"outputs":[{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"Model 2 test MAE (1 month): 1.188 deg C\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0},{"kind":"display_data","png":"cell26_0.png","w":1390,"h":425,"text":"<Figure size 1400x420 with 3 Axes>","mimes":["image/png","text/plain"]}]},
  {"i":27,"type":"markdown","src":"The one-month test MAE is 1.188 degrees. In the three examples the predicted month lands within about three degrees of the target, and the maximum is on the warm side each time (sequence 95: about 28 degrees predicted, 25 recorded), which is the same Bickley bias as model 1 and is picked apart in section 9.\n\nTo forecast 12 months the model is run in a loop: predict month 73, append it to the input, predict month 74 from that, and so on. There are two ways to handle the growing input. Geron's example lets the sequence grow, so the model sees 73, then 74, then up to 83 months. I slide a 72-month window instead, dropping the oldest month each time a prediction is appended, so the model only ever sees inputs of the length it was trained on. The two gave the same 12-month validation MAE for this model when I tried both, so the choice is about safety rather than accuracy: a ReLU variant I tried went badly wrong when its input was allowed to grow (3.4 degrees against 1.1 with the window). The loop never sees ground truth past month 72; everything after that is the model's own output."},
  {"i":28,"type":"code","src":"def predict_recursively(model, X, n_steps):\n    \"\"\"Predict n_steps months one at a time, feeding each prediction back in as the newest month.\n\n    The window always holds the latest INPUT_LENGTH months: the oldest month is dropped\n    and the prediction appended, so the model only ever sees inputs of the length it was trained on.\n    \"\"\"\n    window = X\n    predictions = []\n    for _ in range(n_steps):\n        y_next = model(window, training=False)                    # (N, 1, 2)\n        predictions.append(y_next)\n        window = tf.concat([window[:, 1:], y_next], axis=1)\n    return tf.concat(predictions, axis=1)\n\ny_pred2 = predict_recursively(model2, X_test, TARGET_LENGTH)\nprint(f\"Model 2 test MAE (12 months, recursive): {evaluate_mae(y_test, y_pred2):.3f} deg C\")\ndisplay_temperatures(X_test, y_test, y_pred2, indices=SHOW, title=\"Model 2: 12 months predicted recursively\")","ec":14,"outputs":[{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"Model 2 test MAE (12 months, recursive): 1.407 deg C\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0},{"kind":"display_data","png":"cell28_0.png","w":1390,"h":425,"text":"<Figure size 1400x420 with 3 Axes>","mimes":["image/png","text/plain"]}]},
  {"i":29,"type":"markdown","src":"Rolled out to 12 months the test MAE is 1.407 degrees, up from 1.188 for a single month, which is the cost of feeding predictions back in: each month's error becomes part of the input for the next. It is also worse than model 1's 1.250, so predicting the 12 months jointly beats chaining single-month predictions here. The three examples show the shape of the year surviving the recursion, including the climb into summer in sequence 259, but the predicted curve is smoother than the real one. The winter maximums in sequence 95 are too warm by around 3 degrees again."},
  {"i":30,"type":"markdown","src":"## 8. Model 3: GRU encoder-decoder\n\nModel 3 splits the job in two. The encoder is model 1's GRU stack, two tanh GRU layers of 16 units, trained from scratch here rather than copied from model 1. (I also tried copying model 1's trained layers, both trainable and frozen: free-running validation MAEs of 1.19 and 1.18 against 1.16 from scratch, within seed noise, so I kept the simpler version.) Instead of a Dense layer on the final state, the encoder hands over the final hidden state of each of its two layers. The decoder is another two GRU(16) layers: the first starts from encoder layer 1's final state and the second from encoder layer 2's, which in Keras terms is return_state=True on the encoder layers and initial_state on the decoder layers. The decoder layers return their full sequence and a Dense layer of 2 units turns each step's output into that month's minimum and maximum. Both the encoder and decoder inputs go through the same scaling layer and the output through the inverse one.\n\nTraining uses teacher forcing. The decoder is given the real previous month at every step: its input is the last input month (month 72) followed by the first 11 target months, and its target is the 12 target months. At step k it reads the true month 72 + k - 1 and has to produce month 72 + k. This makes training a single forward pass over 12 steps and lets fit handle it like any other model. The training model has 5,218 parameters, the encoder's 2,592 plus the decoder's 2,626.\n\nAt test time the ground truth is not available, so the decoder has to run on its own output. For that I build two more Keras models from the same layer objects, so they share the trained weights: an encoder that returns the two states, and a one-step decoder that takes one month and the two states and returns the next month and the updated states. predict_encoder_decoder encodes the 72 months, starts the decoder from month 72, and loops n_steps times, feeding each predicted month and both states straight back in. Nothing from y_test enters the loop. The n_steps argument is what section 9 uses to run the decoder past the 12 steps it was trained on.","headings":[{"level":2,"text":"8. Model 3: GRU encoder-decoder","id":"dlp2-h-8-model-3-gru-encoder-decoder"}]},
  {"i":31,"type":"code","src":"def build_encoder_decoder(units=GRU_UNITS):\n    \"\"\"GRU encoder-decoder. Returns the training model plus an inference encoder and decoder.\n\n    The final hidden state of encoder GRU layer 1 initialises decoder GRU layer 1, and likewise\n    for layer 2. The inference decoder takes one month and the two states and returns the\n    next month and the updated states, so it can be run step by step on its own predictions.\n    \"\"\"\n    norm, denorm = scaling_layers()\n    encoder_gru1 = keras.layers.GRU(units, activation=\"tanh\", return_sequences=True, return_state=True,\n                                    name=\"encoder_gru1\")\n    encoder_gru2 = keras.layers.GRU(units, activation=\"tanh\", return_state=True, name=\"encoder_gru2\")\n    decoder_gru1 = keras.layers.GRU(units, activation=\"tanh\", return_sequences=True, return_state=True,\n                                    name=\"decoder_gru1\")\n    decoder_gru2 = keras.layers.GRU(units, activation=\"tanh\", return_sequences=True, return_state=True,\n                                    name=\"decoder_gru2\")\n    output_layer = keras.layers.Dense(2, name=\"next_month\")\n\n    # training model: 72 input months in, 12 decoder input months in, 12 predicted months out\n    encoder_input = keras.Input(shape=(None, 2), name=\"encoder_input\")\n    decoder_input = keras.Input(shape=(None, 2), name=\"decoder_input\")\n    sequence1, state1 = encoder_gru1(norm(encoder_input))\n    _, state2 = encoder_gru2(sequence1)\n    decoded1, _ = decoder_gru1(norm(decoder_input), initial_state=state1)\n    decoded2, _ = decoder_gru2(decoded1, initial_state=state2)\n    model = keras.Model([encoder_input, decoder_input], denorm(output_layer(decoded2)), name=\"model3\")\n\n    # inference models built from the same layers, so they share the trained weights\n    encoder = keras.Model(encoder_input, [state1, state2], name=\"encoder\")\n    month_input = keras.Input(shape=(None, 2), name=\"previous_month\")      # fed one month at a time\n    state1_input = keras.Input(shape=(units,), name=\"state1\")\n    state2_input = keras.Input(shape=(units,), name=\"state2\")\n    step1, new_state1 = decoder_gru1(norm(month_input), initial_state=state1_input)\n    step2, new_state2 = decoder_gru2(step1, initial_state=state2_input)\n    decoder = keras.Model([month_input, state1_input, state2_input],\n                          [denorm(output_layer(step2)), new_state1, new_state2], name=\"decoder\")\n    return model, encoder, decoder\n\ndef decoder_inputs(X, y):\n    \"\"\"Teacher-forcing decoder input: the last input month followed by the targets shifted one month later.\"\"\"\n    return tf.concat([X[:, -1:], y[:, :-1]], axis=1)\n\ndef predict_encoder_decoder(encoder, decoder, X, n_steps):\n    \"\"\"Encode X, then run the decoder one month at a time, feeding back its own prediction and states.\"\"\"\n    state1, state2 = encoder(X, training=False)\n    month = X[:, -1:]                                    # the decoder starts from the last known month\n    predictions = []\n    for _ in range(n_steps):\n        month, state1, state2 = decoder([month, state1, state2], training=False)\n        predictions.append(month)\n    return tf.concat(predictions, axis=1)\n\nmodel3, encoder, decoder = build_encoder_decoder()\nmodel3.summary(line_length=110)       # wide enough to show which state feeds which layer\nencoder.summary(line_length=110)\ndecoder.summary(line_length=110)","ec":15,"outputs":[{"kind":"display_data","text":"Model: \"model3\"\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":"┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓\n┃ Layer (type)                   ┃ Output Shape              ┃          Param # ┃ Connected to               ┃\n┡━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┩\n│ decoder_input (InputLayer)     │ (None, None, 2)           │                0 │ -                          │\n├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤\n│ encoder_input (InputLayer)     │ (None, None, 2)           │                0 │ -                          │\n├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤\n│ normalization_4                │ (None, None, 2)           │                0 │ encoder_input[0][0],       │\n│ (Normalization)                │                           │                  │ decoder_input[0][0]        │\n├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤\n│ encoder_gru1 (GRU)             │ [(None, None, 16), (None, │              960 │ normalization_4[0][0]      │\n│                                │ 16)]                      │                  │                            │\n├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤\n│ decoder_gru1 (GRU)             │ [(None, None, 16), (None, │              960 │ normalization_4[1][0],     │\n│                                │ 16)]                      │                  │ encoder_gru1[0][1]         │\n├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤\n│ encoder_gru2 (GRU)             │ [(None, 16), (None, 16)]  │            1,632 │ encoder_gru1[0][0]         │\n├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤\n│ decoder_gru2 (GRU)             │ [(None, None, 16), (None, │            1,632 │ decoder_gru1[0][0],        │\n│                                │ 16)]                      │                  │ encoder_gru2[0][1]         │\n├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤\n│ next_month (Dense)             │ (None, None, 2)           │               34 │ decoder_gru2[0][0]         │\n├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤\n│ normalization_5                │ (None, None, 2)           │                0 │ next_month[0][0]           │\n│ (Normalization)                │                           │                  │                            │\n└────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────┴──────────────────┴────────────────────────────┘\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Total params: 5,218 (20.38 KB)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Trainable params: 5,218 (20.38 KB)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Non-trainable params: 0 (0.00 B)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":"Model: \"encoder\"\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":"┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓\n┃ Layer (type)                                   ┃ Output Shape                        ┃             Param # ┃\n┡━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┩\n│ encoder_input (InputLayer)                     │ (None, None, 2)                     │                   0 │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────┤\n│ normalization_4 (Normalization)                │ (None, None, 2)                     │                   0 │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────┤\n│ encoder_gru1 (GRU)                             │ [(None, None, 16), (None, 16)]      │                 960 │\n├────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────┤\n│ encoder_gru2 (GRU)                             │ [(None, 16), (None, 16)]            │               1,632 │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────┘\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Total params: 2,592 (10.12 KB)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Trainable params: 2,592 (10.12 KB)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Non-trainable params: 0 (0.00 B)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":"Model: \"decoder\"\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":"┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓\n┃ Layer (type)                   ┃ Output Shape              ┃          Param # ┃ Connected to               ┃\n┡━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┩\n│ previous_month (InputLayer)    │ (None, None, 2)           │                0 │ -                          │\n├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤\n│ normalization_4                │ (None, None, 2)           │                0 │ previous_month[0][0]       │\n│ (Normalization)                │                           │                  │                            │\n├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤\n│ state1 (InputLayer)            │ (None, 16)                │                0 │ -                          │\n├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤\n│ decoder_gru1 (GRU)             │ [(None, None, 16), (None, │              960 │ normalization_4[2][0],     │\n│                                │ 16)]                      │                  │ state1[0][0]               │\n├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤\n│ state2 (InputLayer)            │ (None, 16)                │                0 │ -                          │\n├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤\n│ decoder_gru2 (GRU)             │ [(None, None, 16), (None, │            1,632 │ decoder_gru1[1][0],        │\n│                                │ 16)]                      │                  │ state2[0][0]               │\n├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤\n│ next_month (Dense)             │ (None, None, 2)           │               34 │ decoder_gru2[1][0]         │\n├────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤\n│ normalization_5                │ (None, None, 2)           │                0 │ next_month[1][0]           │\n│ (Normalization)                │                           │                  │                            │\n└────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────┴──────────────────┴────────────────────────────┘\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Total params: 2,626 (10.26 KB)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Trainable params: 2,626 (10.26 KB)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Non-trainable params: 0 (0.00 B)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]}]},
  {"i":32,"type":"code","src":"history3 = train_model(model3, [X_train, decoder_inputs(X_train, y_train)], y_train,\n                       [X_val, decoder_inputs(X_val, y_val)], y_val)\nplot_learning_curve(history3, title=\"Model 3 (teacher forcing)\")","ec":16,"outputs":[{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"stopped after 50 epochs (64 s), kept the weights from epoch 30: val_loss 1.4601, val_mae 0.962\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0},{"kind":"display_data","png":"cell32_0.png","w":1090,"h":377,"text":"<Figure size 1100x380 with 2 Axes>","mimes":["image/png","text/plain"]}]},
  {"i":33,"type":"markdown","src":"Early stopping cut model 3 off at epoch 50 and kept the weights from epoch 30, where the validation loss was 1.46 degrees squared and the validation MAE 0.962 degrees. The training loss falls to 2 in two epochs and then slowly to 1.35; the validation loss follows it down more gradually, is level with it from about epoch 20, bottoms out at 30 and then drifts up to around 1.7 while training keeps falling. There is the same mild overfitting after the best epoch as in model 1, handled the same way.\n\nThe important caveat is that these curves are teacher-forced. Every decoder step was given the true previous month, so the losses measure how well the model predicts one month ahead given a perfect history, not how well it forecasts 12 months on its own. On the validation set the teacher-forced MAE at the best epoch is 0.962 degrees while the free-running MAE reported in section 9 is 1.178, so the curves are optimistic by about 0.2 degrees. In a tuning run that recorded the free-running validation MAE after every epoch, the epoch early stopping picked was within 0.04 degrees of the best free-running epoch, so stopping on the teacher-forced val_loss is adequate even though the number it watches is optimistic."},
  {"i":34,"type":"code","src":"y_pred3 = predict_encoder_decoder(encoder, decoder, X_test, TARGET_LENGTH)\nprint(f\"Model 3 test MAE (12 months, decoder fed its own predictions): {evaluate_mae(y_test, y_pred3):.3f} deg C\")\ndisplay_temperatures(X_test, y_test, y_pred3, indices=SHOW, title=\"Model 3: 12-month predictions on the test set\")","ec":17,"outputs":[{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"Model 3 test MAE (12 months, decoder fed its own predictions): 1.321 deg C\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0},{"kind":"display_data","png":"cell34_0.png","w":1390,"h":425,"text":"<Figure size 1400x420 with 3 Axes>","mimes":["image/png","text/plain"]}]},
  {"i":35,"type":"markdown","src":"With the decoder running on its own predictions the test MAE is 1.321 degrees: better than model 2's recursive 1.407 but worse than model 1's 1.250. The three examples look much like model 1's, with the annual shape right and the summer maximums close, and the same warm winter maximums at Bickley. Sequence 95's winter trough goes wrong in the same way as for models 1 and 2, predicted at 18 to 20 degrees against the recorded 14 to 16, and its last month finishes high for all three models."},
  {"i":36,"type":"markdown","src":"## 9. Comparing models 2 and 3 over longer prediction horizons\n\nNothing is retrained here. For each horizon h, split_sequences cuts the test sequences into the usual 72 input months and the next h months, which for h = 24 uses all 96 months, and the two models are run for h steps, model 2 through predict_recursively and model 3 through predict_encoder_decoder. Two baselines go on the same plot to give the MAEs a scale. Seasonal naive predicts each month as the same month one year earlier, by repeating the last 12 input months. Climatology predicts each calendar month as its mean over the six input years, so it is the smooth average year of that station. Neither baseline has any parameters and both only ever look at the station's own 72 input months.\n\nThe second cell looks at where the error comes from: the MAE for each lead month at the 24-month horizon, and then the validation-against-test MAEs at 12 months together with each channel's MAE and mean signed error (predicted minus actual), which is the bias.","headings":[{"level":2,"text":"9. Comparing models 2 and 3 over longer prediction horizons","id":"dlp2-h-9-comparing-models-2-and-3-over-longer-prediction-horizons"}]},
  {"i":37,"type":"code","src":"HORIZONS = [12, 15, 18, 21, 24]\n\ndef seasonal_naive(X, n_steps):\n    \"\"\"Baseline: repeat the last 12 input months (each month is predicted as the same month a year earlier).\"\"\"\n    repeats = -(-n_steps // 12)\n    return tf.tile(X[:, -12:], [1, repeats, 1])[:, :n_steps]\n\ndef climatology(X, n_steps):\n    \"\"\"Baseline: repeat the average year of the input (each calendar month's mean over the input years).\"\"\"\n    years = X.shape[1] // 12\n    average_year = tf.reduce_mean(tf.reshape(X, (-1, years, 12, 2)), axis=1)\n    repeats = -(-n_steps // 12)\n    return tf.tile(average_year, [1, repeats, 1])[:, :n_steps]\n\nmae_by_horizon = {\"model 2\": [], \"model 3\": [], \"seasonal naive\": [], \"climatology\": []}\nfor h in HORIZONS:\n    X_h, y_h = split_sequences(test_seq, INPUT_LENGTH, h)      # brings in months 85 to 96 as needed\n    mae_by_horizon[\"model 2\"].append(evaluate_mae(y_h, predict_recursively(model2, X_h, h)))\n    mae_by_horizon[\"model 3\"].append(evaluate_mae(y_h, predict_encoder_decoder(encoder, decoder, X_h, h)))\n    mae_by_horizon[\"seasonal naive\"].append(evaluate_mae(y_h, seasonal_naive(X_h, h)))\n    mae_by_horizon[\"climatology\"].append(evaluate_mae(y_h, climatology(X_h, h)))\n    print(f\"horizon {h:2d}: \" + \", \".join(f\"{k} {v[-1]:.3f}\" for k, v in mae_by_horizon.items()) + \" deg C\")\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 4))\nfor name, maes in mae_by_horizon.items():\n    ax.plot(HORIZONS, maes, marker=\"o\", ls=\"-\" if \"model\" in name else \"--\", label=name)\nax.set(xlabel=\"prediction horizon (months)\", ylabel=\"test MAE (deg C)\", xticks=HORIZONS,\n       title=\"Test MAE against prediction horizon\")\nax.legend()\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","ec":18,"outputs":[{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"horizon 12: model 2 1.407, model 3 1.321, seasonal naive 1.235, climatology 0.958 deg C\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0},{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"horizon 15: model 2 1.422, model 3 1.343, seasonal naive 1.233, climatology 0.959 deg C\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0},{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"horizon 18: model 2 1.452, model 3 1.374, seasonal naive 1.231, climatology 0.958 deg C\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0},{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"horizon 21: model 2 1.468, model 3 1.417, seasonal naive 1.232, climatology 0.958 deg C\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0},{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"horizon 24: model 2 1.470, model 3 1.457, seasonal naive 1.234, climatology 0.957 deg C\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0},{"kind":"display_data","png":"cell37_0.png","w":690,"h":390,"text":"<Figure size 700x400 with 1 Axes>","mimes":["image/png","text/plain"]}]},
  {"i":38,"type":"code","src":"# error for each month ahead, averaged over the 297 sequences and both channels, at the 24-month horizon\nX_24, y_24 = split_sequences(test_seq, INPUT_LENGTH, 24)\nmonths = np.arange(1, 25)\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 4))\nfor name, y_hat in [(\"model 2\", predict_recursively(model2, X_24, 24)),\n                    (\"model 3\", predict_encoder_decoder(encoder, decoder, X_24, 24)),\n                    (\"seasonal naive\", seasonal_naive(X_24, 24)),\n                    (\"climatology\", climatology(X_24, 24))]:\n    ax.plot(months, tf.reduce_mean(tf.abs(y_24 - y_hat), axis=(0, 2)), marker=\".\",\n            ls=\"-\" if \"model\" in name else \"--\", label=name)\nax.axvline(12.5, color=\"grey\", lw=0.8)\nax.set(xlabel=\"months ahead\", ylabel=\"test MAE (deg C)\", title=\"Error by lead month (24-month horizon)\")\nax.legend()\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\n# how well do the patterns transfer: validation (Swanbourne) vs test (Bickley) at 12 months,\n# then the test MAE and the mean test error (predicted minus actual) for each channel\nprint(\"12-month MAE on validation and test, then per-channel test MAE and bias (deg C):\")\nfor name, y_val_hat, y_test_hat in [\n        (\"model 1\", model1(X_val, training=False), y_pred1),\n        (\"model 2\", predict_recursively(model2, X_val, 12), y_pred2),\n        (\"model 3\", predict_encoder_decoder(encoder, decoder, X_val, 12), y_pred3),\n        (\"seasonal naive\", seasonal_naive(X_val, 12), seasonal_naive(X_test, 12)),\n        (\"climatology\", climatology(X_val, 12), climatology(X_test, 12))]:\n    mae_ch = tf.reduce_mean(tf.abs(y_test_hat - y_test), axis=(0, 1))\n    bias = tf.reduce_mean(y_test_hat - y_test, axis=(0, 1))\n    print(f\"  {name:15s} val {evaluate_mae(y_val, y_val_hat):.3f}  test {evaluate_mae(y_test, y_test_hat):.3f}  |  \"\n          f\"min: MAE {mae_ch[0]:.2f}, bias {bias[0]:+.2f}   max: MAE {mae_ch[1]:.2f}, bias {bias[1]:+.2f}\")","ec":19,"outputs":[{"kind":"display_data","png":"cell38_0.png","w":689,"h":390,"text":"<Figure size 700x400 with 1 Axes>","mimes":["image/png","text/plain"]},{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"12-month MAE on validation and test, then per-channel test MAE and bias (deg C):\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0},{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"  model 1         val 0.988  test 1.250  |  min: MAE 0.79, bias -0.08   max: MAE 1.71, bias +0.81\n  model 2         val 0.982  test 1.407  |  min: MAE 0.97, bias +0.47   max: MAE 1.84, bias +1.14\n  model 3         val 1.178  test 1.321  |  min: MAE 0.87, bias -0.08   max: MAE 1.77, bias +1.20\n  seasonal naive  val 1.226  test 1.235  |  min: MAE 1.04, bias -0.05   max: MAE 1.43, bias -0.06\n  climatology     val 0.967  test 0.958  |  min: MAE 0.82, bias -0.10   max: MAE 1.10, bias -0.11\n","lines":5,"noise":false,"tfLines":0}]},
  {"i":39,"type":"markdown","src":"### Discussion\n\nBoth models get worse as the horizon grows and model 3 deteriorates faster. Its MAE rises from 1.321 degrees at 12 months to 1.457 at 24, an increase of 0.136, while model 2 goes from 1.407 to 1.470, an increase of 0.063, so model 3 starts well ahead and has nearly lost its lead by 24 months.\n\nThe horizon MAE is an average over every month up to the horizon, which hides how bad the last months are, and the lead-month plot shows the real picture. Model 3's error climbs from 1.10 degrees one month out to about 1.4 by month 12, holds there for a few months, and then rises steeply to about 1.75 at 22 months. Its decoder was only ever trained to run for 12 steps, so months 13 to 24 push the hidden state past anything it saw in training, and that is where its error grows fastest. Model 2's error rises quickly over the first six months to about 1.48, then wobbles between about 1.4 and 1.6 with a 12-month rhythm instead of climbing further. Its sliding window keeps feeding it 72 months of plausible data, so it settles into repeating a slightly wrong year rather than drifting away. Month by month the two are level around months 11 to 13, and from month 19 on model 3 is the worse of the two, so its lead in the cumulative MAE at 24 months is only what it banked in the first year. One step out, model 3's decoder is the better of the two (1.10 compared with 1.19), which I think is the two-layer state carrying more than model 2's single Dense readout.\n\nThe patterns transfer poorly to Bickley. On Swanbourne, the validation station, models 1 and 2 are level with the climatology baseline (0.988 and 0.982 degrees against 0.967) and model 3 is 0.2 behind it. On Bickley every model is worse than both baselines, 1.25 to 1.41 degrees where seasonal naive gets 1.235 and climatology 0.96, and the plot shows the models above both baseline lines at every horizon.\n\nThe per-channel numbers say where the problem is. The minimum channel is fine: models 1 and 3 have a bias of -0.08 degrees and MAEs of 0.79 and 0.87, about the same as climatology's 0.82. The maximum channel is not. All three models over-predict Bickley's maximums by 0.8 to 1.2 degrees on average and their maximum-channel MAEs of 1.7 to 1.85 are far above climatology's 1.10. Bickley's maximums average 2.1 degrees cooler than the training stations', and from the plots the models get its summer peaks about right but not its winter troughs. So they only take about half of the difference from the input window and fill in the rest from what they learned on the training stations. The baselines copy the station's own history and so have no bias to speak of (climatology -0.10 and -0.11 on the two channels, seasonal naive -0.05 and -0.06). Model 2 is the worst transfer case, with a bias on both channels, because its errors compound through the recursion.\n\nI think this is a limitation of the design rather than of the training. The models are given absolute temperatures and have to infer a station's level from 72 months, but the three training stations only cover a narrow band of levels, so a cooler station is extrapolation. A version that predicts each month's deviation from the input window's own average year, with that average year added back afterwards, would make the models level-invariant and is the first thing I would try next. It would also give them the climatology baseline as a starting point rather than something to beat. I did not switch to it here because the decision would have been driven by the test set.\n\nOn the two questions asked: model 3 deteriorates faster with the horizon. Both models learn the shape of the annual cycle well enough, but they carry the warmth of the training stations with them, and on a station with a different climate that makes them worse than a plain average of the station's own past.","headings":[{"level":3,"text":"Discussion","id":"dlp2-h-discussion"}]},
  {"i":40,"type":"markdown","src":"# Part (ii): Synthesising maximum temperature sequences with a VAE\n\nA variational autoencoder is trained on the 84-month maximum temperature sequences and then used to make up new ones, which are compared with the real data.","headings":[{"level":1,"text":"Part (ii): Synthesising maximum temperature sequences with a VAE","id":"dlp2-h-part-ii-synthesising-maximum-temperature-sequences-with-a-va"}]},
  {"i":41,"type":"markdown","src":"## 10. Data preparation\n\nThe VAE sees only the maximum temperature channel, so the inputs are the full 84-month sequences from train_seq[:, :, 1], 1522 of them, with the 313 Swanbourne sequences as the validation set. I standardise them with one mean and standard deviation over all training values (24.70 and 5.11 degrees), the same fixed affine scaling as in part (i) but for one channel. The decoder's linear output is then in z-score units, so the reconstruction MSE is on a scale the KL term can be weighed against. Everything is converted back to degrees for plotting and for the statistics in section 12. Nothing else is done to the data; the annual cycle and the month-to-month noise are what the VAE has to learn.\n\nThe assumptions behind this are that an 84-month window of maximums from any of the three training stations is a sample from one shared process, that a handful of numbers is enough to describe such a window, and that a standard normal prior is a reasonable distribution for those numbers.","headings":[{"level":2,"text":"10. Data preparation","id":"dlp2-h-10-data-preparation"}]},
  {"i":42,"type":"code","src":"SEQ_LENGTH = 84\n\n# channel 1 is the monthly mean maximum; the VAE works on z-scores of the whole 84-month sequence\nx_train_max = train_seq[:, :, 1]\nx_val_max = val_seq[:, :, 1]\nMAX_MEAN = tf.reduce_mean(x_train_max)\nMAX_STD = tf.math.reduce_std(x_train_max)\nz_train = (x_train_max - MAX_MEAN) / MAX_STD\nz_val = (x_val_max - MAX_MEAN) / MAX_STD\nprint(f\"train {tuple(z_train.shape)}, val {tuple(z_val.shape)}; \"\n      f\"training max-temperature mean {MAX_MEAN:.2f} and std {MAX_STD:.2f} deg C\")","ec":20,"outputs":[{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"train (1522, 84), val (313, 84); training max-temperature mean 24.70 and std 5.11 deg C\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0}]},
  {"i":43,"type":"markdown","src":"## 11. VAE model\n\nCodings size. An 84-month maximum temperature sequence is close to a fixed annual cycle, so the things a code has to describe are the overall level, the amplitude of the cycle, the phase (which calendar month the window starts in, a circular quantity that needs two numbers) and some year-to-year wobble. That suggests about four to eight numbers. I tried 2, 3, 4, 6, 8 and 16 and in every case the model used between two and five of the dimensions, so I settled on 4 and print the KL per dimension after training to show how many are actually in use.\n\nArchitecture. I went with convolutions rather than Dense layers because the signal is local and repeating: a Conv1D filter that recognises a summer peak can be reused seven times along the sequence. The encoder reshapes the 84 months to (84, 1), applies Conv1D(16) and Conv1D(32), both with kernel 5 and stride 2, so the sequence shrinks to 42 and then 21 steps, flattens, and two Dense heads produce the 4 means and 4 log-variances. The decoder mirrors it: a Dense layer expands the code to (21, 32), then Conv1DTranspose(16) and Conv1DTranspose(1) with the same kernel and stride bring it back to 84 months. The hidden layers use ReLU, which has no saturation to worry about on z-scored inputs and trains quickly, and the output is linear since the targets are z-scores that can be negative. The Dense version I compared it with reconstructed slightly worse in the tuning runs (about 0.95 degrees on validation, where this architecture gave 0.93; the final run below gives 0.940).\n\nSampling and loss. The Sampling layer implements the reparameterisation trick, z = mean + exp(log_var / 2) * epsilon with epsilon drawn from N(0, I), so the randomness sits outside the gradient path. The same layer adds the KL divergence to the model's losses with add_loss, which is the Keras 3 way of doing what Geron does with add_loss on the model. The KL is summed over the 4 dimensions and averaged over the batch, then weighted: I divide by 84 so that it is per month like the MSE (Geron divides by the 784 pixels of an MNIST image for the same reason) and then halve it.\n\nAt the full per-month weight only two latent dimensions stayed active whatever the codings size, and with the Dense model every sample came out at the same mean level. At half the weight a third dimension is used, the samples spread in level like the real data, and the validation reconstruction MAE of this architecture improved from 0.95 to 0.93 degrees when I tried both weights. Going lower again, to a quarter, freed a fourth dimension but produced more of the degenerate flat samples discussed in section 12, so a half is the compromise.\n\nTraining reuses train_model with the loss set to MSE and the metric to MSE, so the learning curve shows both the total loss (reconstruction plus the weighted KL) and the reconstruction part on its own. Early stopping watches the total validation loss, which I checked does include the KL term.","headings":[{"level":2,"text":"11. VAE model","id":"dlp2-h-11-vae-model"}]},
  {"i":44,"type":"code","src":"CODINGS_SIZE = 4\nKL_WEIGHT = 0.5 / SEQ_LENGTH      # KL per time step, then halved\n\nclass Sampling(keras.layers.Layer):\n    \"\"\"Draw a codings vector z = mean + exp(log_var / 2) * epsilon (the reparameterisation trick)\n    and add the KL divergence between N(mean, var) and N(0, I) to the model's losses.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, kl_weight, **kwargs):\n        super().__init__(**kwargs)\n        self.kl_weight = kl_weight\n\n    def call(self, inputs):\n        z_mean, z_log_var = inputs\n        epsilon = keras.random.normal(shape=tf.shape(z_mean))\n        kl = -0.5 * tf.reduce_sum(1 + z_log_var - tf.exp(z_log_var) - tf.square(z_mean), axis=-1)\n        self.add_loss(self.kl_weight * tf.reduce_mean(kl))\n        return z_mean + tf.exp(0.5 * z_log_var) * epsilon\n\ndef build_vae(codings_size=CODINGS_SIZE, kl_weight=KL_WEIGHT):\n    \"\"\"Conv1D encoder and Conv1DTranspose decoder for 84-month sequences. Returns (vae, encoder, decoder).\"\"\"\n    # encoder: 84 months -> 42 x 16 -> 21 x 32 -> codings\n    inputs = keras.Input(shape=(SEQ_LENGTH,))\n    x = keras.layers.Reshape((SEQ_LENGTH, 1))(inputs)\n    x = keras.layers.Conv1D(16, 5, strides=2, padding=\"same\", activation=\"relu\")(x)\n    x = keras.layers.Conv1D(32, 5, strides=2, padding=\"same\", activation=\"relu\")(x)\n    x = keras.layers.Flatten()(x)\n    z_mean = keras.layers.Dense(codings_size, name=\"z_mean\")(x)\n    z_log_var = keras.layers.Dense(codings_size, name=\"z_log_var\")(x)\n    z = Sampling(kl_weight, name=\"sampling\")([z_mean, z_log_var])\n    encoder = keras.Model(inputs, [z_mean, z_log_var, z], name=\"encoder\")\n\n    # decoder: codings -> 21 x 32 -> 42 x 16 -> 84 months (linear output, z-score units)\n    codings = keras.Input(shape=(codings_size,))\n    y = keras.layers.Dense(21 * 32, activation=\"relu\")(codings)\n    y = keras.layers.Reshape((21, 32))(y)\n    y = keras.layers.Conv1DTranspose(16, 5, strides=2, padding=\"same\", activation=\"relu\")(y)\n    y = keras.layers.Conv1DTranspose(1, 5, strides=2, padding=\"same\")(y)\n    outputs = keras.layers.Reshape((SEQ_LENGTH,))(y)\n    decoder = keras.Model(codings, outputs, name=\"decoder\")\n\n    vae = keras.Model(inputs, decoder(z), name=\"vae\")\n    return vae, encoder, decoder\n\nvae, vae_encoder, vae_decoder = build_vae()\nvae_encoder.summary()\nvae_decoder.summary()","ec":21,"outputs":[{"kind":"display_data","text":"Model: \"encoder\"\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":"┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓\n┃ Layer (type)        ┃ Output Shape      ┃    Param # ┃ Connected to      ┃\n┡━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┩\n│ input_layer_2       │ (None, 84)        │          0 │ -                 │\n│ (InputLayer)        │                   │            │                   │\n├─────────────────────┼───────────────────┼────────────┼───────────────────┤\n│ reshape_2 (Reshape) │ (None, 84, 1)     │          0 │ input_layer_2[0]… │\n├─────────────────────┼───────────────────┼────────────┼───────────────────┤\n│ conv1d (Conv1D)     │ (None, 42, 16)    │         96 │ reshape_2[0][0]   │\n├─────────────────────┼───────────────────┼────────────┼───────────────────┤\n│ conv1d_1 (Conv1D)   │ (None, 21, 32)    │      2,592 │ conv1d[0][0]      │\n├─────────────────────┼───────────────────┼────────────┼───────────────────┤\n│ flatten (Flatten)   │ (None, 672)       │          0 │ conv1d_1[0][0]    │\n├─────────────────────┼───────────────────┼────────────┼───────────────────┤\n│ z_mean (Dense)      │ (None, 4)         │      2,692 │ flatten[0][0]     │\n├─────────────────────┼───────────────────┼────────────┼───────────────────┤\n│ z_log_var (Dense)   │ (None, 4)         │      2,692 │ flatten[0][0]     │\n├─────────────────────┼───────────────────┼────────────┼───────────────────┤\n│ sampling (Sampling) │ (None, 4)         │          0 │ z_mean[0][0],     │\n│                     │                   │            │ z_log_var[0][0]   │\n└─────────────────────┴───────────────────┴────────────┴───────────────────┘\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Total params: 8,072 (31.53 KB)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Trainable params: 8,072 (31.53 KB)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Non-trainable params: 0 (0.00 B)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":"Model: \"decoder\"\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":"┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┳━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓\n┃ Layer (type)                    ┃ Output Shape           ┃       Param # ┃\n┡━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━╇━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┩\n│ input_layer_3 (InputLayer)      │ (None, 4)              │             0 │\n├─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤\n│ dense_2 (Dense)                 │ (None, 672)            │         3,360 │\n├─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤\n│ reshape_3 (Reshape)             │ (None, 21, 32)         │             0 │\n├─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤\n│ conv1d_transpose                │ (None, 42, 16)         │         2,576 │\n│ (Conv1DTranspose)               │                        │               │\n├─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤\n│ conv1d_transpose_1              │ (None, 84, 1)          │            81 │\n│ (Conv1DTranspose)               │                        │               │\n├─────────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┼───────────────┤\n│ reshape_4 (Reshape)             │ (None, 84)             │             0 │\n└─────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────┴───────────────┘\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Total params: 6,017 (23.50 KB)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Trainable params: 6,017 (23.50 KB)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","text":" Non-trainable params: 0 (0.00 B)\n","mimes":["text/html","text/plain"]}]},
  {"i":45,"type":"code","src":"history_vae = train_model(vae, z_train, z_train, z_val, z_val, metrics=(\"mse\",))\nplot_learning_curve(history_vae, title=\"VAE\", metric=\"mse\", metric_label=\"reconstruction MSE (z-score units)\",\n                    loss_label=\"reconstruction MSE + weighted KL\")\n\n# how much of the codings space is actually used: KL per latent dimension on the validation set\nz_mean_val, z_log_var_val, _ = vae_encoder(z_val, training=False)\nkl_per_dim = tf.reduce_mean(-0.5 * (1 + z_log_var_val - tf.exp(z_log_var_val) - tf.square(z_mean_val)), axis=0)\nprint(\"KL per latent dimension (nats):\", kl_per_dim.numpy().round(2))\nrecon_val = vae_decoder(z_mean_val, training=False) * MAX_STD + MAX_MEAN\nprint(f\"validation reconstruction MAE: {evaluate_mae(x_val_max, recon_val):.3f} deg C\")","ec":22,"outputs":[{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"stopped after 43 epochs (32 s), kept the weights from epoch 23: val_loss 0.0950, val_mse 0.067\n","lines":1,"noise":false,"tfLines":0},{"kind":"display_data","png":"cell45_0.png","w":1090,"h":377,"text":"<Figure size 1100x380 with 2 Axes>","mimes":["image/png","text/plain"]},{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"KL per latent dimension (nats): [0.   2.   0.75 1.88]\nvalidation reconstruction MAE: 0.940 deg C\n","lines":2,"noise":false,"tfLines":0}]},
  {"i":46,"type":"markdown","src":"The VAE ran for 43 epochs. The best total validation loss, 0.0950, came at epoch 23, with the reconstruction part at 0.067 in z-score units. Both curves drop steeply in the first few epochs and are flat from about epoch 10. In the total-loss panel the validation curve runs level with or a touch below training until the mid 30s and only climbs above it in the last few epochs; in the reconstruction panel it creeps above training from about epoch 20. That is very mild overfitting and nothing more, about what I would expect from a model that has to squeeze each sequence into 4 numbers. The validation curve is bumpy because the validation pass samples z as well, so the reconstruction error carries some noise from epoch to epoch.\n\nThe KL per dimension shows which of the 4 codings are in use: two carry about 1.9 to 2.0 nats each, one carries 0.75 and one is dead at 0.00, meaning the encoder leaves it at the prior and the decoder ignores it. So the model found three numbers enough, one fewer than the level, amplitude and two-dimensional phase picture suggests. My guess is that the three are the level and the two phase numbers, and that amplitude is not coded on its own but comes out of how far from the origin the phase pair sits, which would also explain the flat samples in the next section.\n\nThe validation reconstruction MAE, decoding each sequence from its mean code, is 0.940 degrees, a little above model 2's one-month validation error of 0.882 on the same station, and it gives an idea of how much the code keeps: the level and the cycle, not the month-to-month noise."},
  {"i":47,"type":"markdown","src":"## 12. Synthesised sequences\n\nSix codings vectors are drawn from N(0, I), decoded and scaled back to degrees. The second figure shows six real training sequences on the same axes for comparison. The cell then compares 500 samples with all 1522 real sequences on five statistics: mean level, spread of levels between sequences, peak-to-trough range, the share with a range under 10 degrees, and the average month-to-month change.","headings":[{"level":2,"text":"12. Synthesised sequences","id":"dlp2-h-12-synthesised-sequences"}]},
  {"i":48,"type":"code","src":"def plot_sequences(sequences, title, ncols=3):\n    \"\"\"Plot 84-month max-temperature sequences (deg C) in a grid of small subplots with shared axes.\"\"\"\n    nrows = -(-len(sequences) // ncols)\n    fig, axes = plt.subplots(nrows, ncols, figsize=(15, 3 * nrows), sharex=True, sharey=True)\n    months = np.arange(1, SEQ_LENGTH + 1)\n    for k, (ax, seq) in enumerate(zip(axes.flat, sequences)):\n        ax.plot(months, np.asarray(seq), color=\"tab:red\", lw=1)\n        ax.set_title(f\"sequence {k + 1}\")\n        if k % ncols == 0:\n            ax.set_ylabel(\"max temperature (deg C)\")\n        if k >= len(sequences) - ncols:\n            ax.set_xlabel(\"month\")\n    fig.suptitle(title)\n    plt.tight_layout()\n    plt.show()\n\n# 6 codings vectors from the N(0, I) prior, decoded and scaled back to deg C\ncodings_sample = keras.random.normal(shape=(6, CODINGS_SIZE))\nsynthetic = vae_decoder(codings_sample, training=False) * MAX_STD + MAX_MEAN\nplot_sequences(synthetic, \"Six synthesised 84-month maximum temperature sequences\")\n\nreal_indices = np.random.choice(len(x_train_max), size=6, replace=False)\nplot_sequences(tf.gather(x_train_max, real_indices), \"Six real training sequences for comparison\")\n\n# compare simple statistics of a larger batch of samples with the real training sequences\nmany = vae_decoder(keras.random.normal(shape=(500, CODINGS_SIZE)), training=False) * MAX_STD + MAX_MEAN\nfor name, seqs in [(\"500 synthesised\", many), (\"all real training\", x_train_max)]:\n    seqs = np.asarray(seqs)\n    ranges = seqs.max(axis=1) - seqs.min(axis=1)\n    print(f\"{name:18s} mean {seqs.mean():.1f}, std of sequence means {seqs.mean(axis=1).std():.2f}, \"\n          f\"range per sequence {ranges.mean():.1f} (min {ranges.min():.1f}), \"\n          f\"{np.mean(ranges < 10):.0%} with range under 10, \"\n          f\"month-to-month change {np.abs(np.diff(seqs, axis=1)).mean():.2f} deg C\")","ec":23,"outputs":[{"kind":"display_data","png":"cell48_0.png","w":1490,"h":593,"text":"<Figure size 1500x600 with 6 Axes>","mimes":["image/png","text/plain"]},{"kind":"display_data","png":"cell48_1.png","w":1490,"h":593,"text":"<Figure size 1500x600 with 6 Axes>","mimes":["image/png","text/plain"]},{"kind":"stream","name":"stdout","text":"500 synthesised    mean 24.5, std of sequence means 0.62, range per sequence 12.6 (min 1.8), 21% with range under 10, month-to-month change 2.03 deg C\nall real training  mean 24.7, std of sequence means 0.56, range per sequence 17.2 (min 14.4), 0% with range under 10, month-to-month change 2.48 deg C\n","lines":2,"noise":false,"tfLines":0}]},
  {"i":49,"type":"markdown","src":"### Discussion\n\nThey are partly realistic. The six samples have the right basic shape: seven annual cycles in 84 months with the peaks about 12 months apart, and maximums between about 16 and 35 degrees, which is the range of the real data. They differ from each other in phase (sequence 1 starts near a summer peak, sequence 5 in a winter trough), in level and in amplitude (sequence 2 swings between 16 and 35, sequence 6 between 19 and 29). The statistics of the larger batch show how the mix compares with the real data:\n\n| | 500 samples | all real training sequences |\n|---|---|---|\n| mean level (deg C) | 24.5 | 24.7 |\n| spread of sequence means | 0.62 | 0.56 |\n| peak-to-trough range | 12.6 | 17.2 |\n| share with a range under 10 degrees | 21% | 0% |\n| month-to-month change | 2.03 | 2.48 |\n\nThe levels are right, so the mix of warmer and cooler windows is about what the stations produce. Two things give the samples away, though. First, they are too smooth. Real sequences have jagged peaks, single hot months that stick out, double-humped summers and the odd mild winter; the samples are clean waves, with a range 4.6 degrees smaller than the real average and smaller steps from month to month. This is the usual VAE blur: with an MSE reconstruction loss the decoder learns the average sequence for a given code, and the month-to-month noise averages out. Even among the six shown in this run, sequences 3 and 6 swing through only about 10 to 11 degrees, less than any real training sequence (the smallest real range is 14.4).\n\nSecond, some draws from the prior are degenerate. One in five of the 500 samples has a range under 10 degrees, and the flattest is almost a straight line with a range of 1.8. I think this is the phase being circular: the encoder maps the start month onto a ring in two latent dimensions, and codes near the middle of the ring, which the prior produces often, decode to the average over all phases, which is a flat line; I have not checked this by plotting the codes. A weaker KL weight made the flat samples more common and a stronger one collapsed the level dimension, so it looks like a limitation of this setup rather than something tuning would fix.\n\nThe VAE has learned the climatology of a Perth maximum temperature series, with a believable spread of levels and phases and a somewhat squashed spread of amplitudes, but not the weather on top of it. To synthesise rough-looking data the next step would be to add noise around the decoded mean, or to give the decoder more capacity near the output.","headings":[{"level":3,"text":"Discussion","id":"dlp2-h-discussion-2"}]},
  {"i":50,"type":"markdown","src":"## 13. Summary\n\n| | Val MAE, 12 months | Test MAE, 12 months | Test MAE, 24 months |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Model 1 (12 months at once) | 0.988 | 1.250 | |\n| Model 2 (1 month, recursive) | 0.982 | 1.407 | 1.470 |\n| Model 3 (encoder-decoder) | 1.178 | 1.321 | 1.457 |\n| Seasonal naive | 1.226 | 1.235 | 1.234 |\n| Climatology | 0.967 | 0.958 | 0.957 |\n\nAll three GRU models learn the annual cycle and forecast Swanbourne about as well as the average of its own past six years, within 0.03 degrees for models 1 and 2. On Bickley they all over-predict the maximums by about a degree and finish behind the two parameter-free baselines, because they carry the warmth of the stations they were trained on. Model 1 was the best of the three on the test set; model 3 beat model 2 at every horizon but deteriorated faster once pushed past the 12 steps it was trained for. The VAE produces smooth, plausibly varied annual cycles at the right level but not the month-to-month roughness of real data, and about a fifth of its prior samples are too flat to pass for real.","headings":[{"level":2,"text":"13. Summary","id":"dlp2-h-13-summary"}]},
];
